  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND STORMS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA weak jet accompanied by some modest moisture will nose into \nthe Four Corners region tonight. Some of the hi-res models \nsupport the development of showers along the moisture transport \ngradient, especially in southern foothills of the San Juans. \nThis moisture remains in place for much of Friday and Saturday, \nespecially along the Divide. As a result, afternoon convection \nfavoring the higher terrain will return to the forecast, but \nmost locales will remain dry with seasonable temperatures and \nbreezy afternoon winds.\n\nMONSOONAL MOISTURE SURGES SUNDAY AND MONDAY: \n\nA strong low pressure over the Pacific Northwest will advect a \nplume of anamolous moisture on Sunday and Monday. This could \nresult in scattered to widespread convection across the region. \nPWATs upwards of 200% of normal will support the potential for \nheavy rainfall and flash flooding, especially around recent burn\nscars and areas with steep terrain. This threat is particularly\nheightened where soils have reached peak saturation after \nmultiple rounds of storms this past week. Models are beginning \nto show that the peak moisture might be overnight Sunday. Also, \nattm on Sunday afternoon there is not much instability or it may\nbe limited to a certain area. This will play a role in the \ncoverage and intensity of showers and storms.\n\nBeyond Monday the low pressure moves across the northern US \ncausing high pressure to build over the region. The uncertainty \nis if we stay in the moist southwest flow. There is also signs \nof an active Pacific Northwest, which may too play a role in \nprecipitation chances.",
